    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In the past few decades, the prevalence of Autism Spectrum Disorders (ASD) has risen to 1 in 68 live births (CDC, 2014), and no lone gene has been named as a single cause of ASD. The 15q11-13 region is a hotspot of ASD-related genes, and found within this area is a gene of interest, GABA-receptor type A beta 3 subunit (GABRB3), implicated in both ASD and innate immune inhibition. Activation of a maternal innate immune response during pregnancy is also a known risk factor for ASD (Croen, Grether, Yoshida, Odouli, & Van de Water, 2005; Mazina et al., 2015). Both Gabrb3 haploinsufficiency and maternal immune activation (MIA), i.e., the exposure to infectious agents resulting in a measurable maternal immune response during gestation, are used in our model. With a mouse model, both factors have been combined at a previously determined critical time-point of placental vulnerability, embryonic day 12.5, to study the combined effects of gene-immune interaction. Immune activation may influence brain development in part by its effects on the placenta, and placental injury is exacerbated by the partial loss of Gabrb3. While previous studies have shown that Gabrb3 deficiency and MIA impact fetal outcome, there is limited work on the expected synergy of combinatorial gene-environment effects or the roles of the placenta in neocortical development. Using lipopolysaccharide (LPS), a Gram-negative bacteria mimic, and Gabrb3-deficient mice, this research explores the combined effects of a gene-environment interaction in relation to ASD. Preliminary results of these experiments suggested the placenta as an important organ in this model of a gene-immune interaction. Novel automated analysis of placental hemorrhage has shown significant variation in the level of visible placental hemorrhage, with saline-treated, wild-type placentas having less hemorrhage than LPS 30-treated, Gabrb3 heterozygous placentas (Figure 7A; P<0.0001; n=158). In addition to this, confirmation of protein levels of GABRB3 in mouse placenta provides greater insights into how Gabrb3 deficiency impacts placental development and can be further exacerbated upon MIA in this model. Understanding this relationship could lead to the elucidation of a novel mechanism underlying ASD development and provide crucial insights into preventative strategies and therapeutic targets.",
